WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.852
Kafka keeps every event in a log.

2
00:00:04.852 --> 00:00:07.983
A log is a list you can only add to, at the end.

3
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.834
Each record gets a number, its offset.

4
00:00:10.834 --> 00:00:12.636
Zero, one, two, and so on.

5
00:00:12.636 --> 00:00:14.855
A record's offset never changes.

6
00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:17.451
A producer only ever appends.

7
00:00:17.451 --> 00:00:20.941
The next payment lands at the end, at offset eight.

8
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:23.091
Reading never removes anything.

9
00:00:23.091 --> 00:00:27.623
Each reader keeps a bookmark: the offset of the next record it will read.

10
00:00:27.833 --> 00:00:30.098
The fraud check is at offset three.

11
00:00:30.098 --> 00:00:32.557
The ledger is further along, at seven.

12
00:00:32.557 --> 00:00:35.469
They read the same log, each at its own pace.

13
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.909
The fraud check reads two records and saves its bookmark: five.

14
00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:41.145
Now it crashes.

15
00:00:41.145 --> 00:00:42.225
Nothing is lost.

16
00:00:42.225 --> 00:00:47.151
The records are still in the log, and the saved bookmark still says five.

17
00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:51.860
It restarts, reads its bookmark, and carries on from offset five.

18
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.588
The ledger never noticed.

19
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.025
The log can't grow forever.

20
00:00:56.025 --> 00:01:02.917
After a retention period, say seven days, Kafka deletes the oldest records, a whole segment at a time.

21
00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:06.314
Offsets don't shift when that happens.

22
00:01:06.314 --> 00:01:11.236
The log now starts at offset four, and every bookmark still means the same record.

23
00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:12.860
That is the whole idea.

24
00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:14.557
Producers append at the end.

25
00:01:14.557 --> 00:01:16.739
Every reader keeps its own bookmark.

26
00:01:16.739 --> 00:01:20.496
And the log keeps records long enough for anyone to come back.
